{"i": "For {proj}, '{req}' would upgrade a framework major version for one new feature. What dependency reasoning sizes this decision?",
 "a": "Size by blast radius: a major upgrade rewrites the runtime contract for every component, so the feature's benefit must outweigh surge-in regression surface plus test-rerun cost. Prefer backports or local workarounds when the delta is small. Majors are migrations, not installs."},
{"i": "In {proj}, '{req}' broke after a transitive dependency of a sub-dependency bumped, invisible in package.json. Where is the reasoning?",
 "a": "Reason at the lockfile: transitive resolution changed underneath frozen direct dependencies, and the lockfile diff names it. Direct manifests do not record transitive movement. Pinning strategy decides who guards the supply chain's middle layers."},
{"i": "For {proj}, '{req}' suffers from two libraries each expecting a different incompatible version of one helper package. What does correct reasoning do?",
 "a": "Do not install both and pray: identify whose APIs are actually invoked, resolve via aliasing or a shared compatible range, and verify in the running app. Version coexistence can work only when usage surfaces do not intersect. Half-solutions at the manifest mutate conflicts into runtime ones."},
{"i": "In {proj}, '{req}' needs removing a deprecated package, and usage search shows zero imports. What check makes removal safe?",
 "a": "Check dynamic and config-driven use: require-by-string, plugin references in build config, template-driven loads, and runtime lookups, since static zero-import proves only static. Zero-imports-plus-runtime-use packages kill production after text-search all-clear. Removal verification includes the running suite."},
{"i": "For {proj}, '{req}' prompts adding a trendy framework package purely for a helper. What reasoning evaluates the choice?",
 "a": "Evaluate total cost of dependency: package weight, security exposure, release cadence, and supply-chain trust against the helper's few lines. Small conveniences re-sold at framework prices rarely settle. A ten-line local utility often wins the ledger."},
